{
  "$schema": "https://biomejs.dev/schemas/2.5.7/schema.json",

  // `lineWidth` is biome's own default of 80, which the doc comments here
  // already wrap near. It is **not** the smallest diff, and the alternatives
  // were measured rather than argued about — 53 of 70 files change here,
  // against 32 at width 100 and 44 at 120:
  //
  //     width   files    diff
  //      80     53/70   +1327 / -396
  //     100     32/70   +422  / -184
  //     120     44/70   +265  / -408
  //
  // 80 splits the 424 non-comment lines that sat at 81-100, which is most of
  // its cost. 120's smaller raw diff is the wrong kind: it mostly *joins*
  // wrapped signatures back into long lines. 80 was chosen for agreeing with
  // the prose already in these files and for being the default, so the number
  // is not up for re-argument later.
  //
  // Measure this with `--max-diagnostics` raised. Biome prints at most 20
  // diagnostics by default, and a naive count of its output reports exactly 20
  // files at every width — a cap that reads convincingly as a result.
  "formatter": {
    "enabled": true,
    "indentStyle": "space",
    "indentWidth": 2,
    "lineWidth": 80
  },

  // Single quotes and no semicolons: not a preference expressed here for the
  // first time, but the one already in the source — 171 imports used `'` and
  // none used `"`, and no statement ended in `;`.
  "javascript": {
    "formatter": {
      "quoteStyle": "single",
      "jsxQuoteStyle": "double",
      "semicolons": "asNeeded"
    }
  },

  "linter": {
    "enabled": true,
    "rules": {
      "recommended": true,

      // **Four rules are off**, none of which oxlint ever flagged. They were
      // turned off together so that adopting biome swapped a tool without
      // also changing what is enforced — fifteen sites, four of which change
      // rendered markup, is a second claim and did not belong in that commit.
      //
      // **Three of them are still open questions and one is not.** For
      // `noNonNullAssertion` and the two `a11y` rules, "off" means nobody has
      // decided yet, and each carries its sites so someone can. For
      // `noArrayIndexKey`, "off" is the decision: its finding was
      // investigated and the code is right. Do not treat the four as one
      // list of chores.
      //
      // They are spread across the three groups below; the one rule turned
      // *on* by name sits among them, marked where it appears.
      "style": {
        // 9 sites. `foo!` assertions, in `main.tsx`'s root lookup, two
        // `Annotations.tsx` handlers, `readouts.ts`, `nearest.ts` and four
        // tests. Each wants its own look at whether the invariant is real.
        "noNonNullAssertion": "off",

        // **On deliberately, and not on by default** — this is the second of
        // the two rules `.oxlintrc.json` enabled by name
        // (`react/only-export-components`), so leaving it at biome's default
        // would have quietly dropped coverage while the branch claimed to
        // change none. Verified by probe rather than by reading the docs: a
        // file exporting both a pure function and a component draws nothing
        // from biome's recommended set and is flagged once this is on.
        // `tiles.ts` exists because of this rule; its doc comment says so.
        "useComponentExportOnlyModules": "error"
      },
      "a11y": {
        // 3 sites, and the fix is markup rather than a suppression:
        // `NoteList.tsx`'s `<div role="list">` wants to be a `<ul>` and
        // `NotePanel.tsx`'s `<div role="group">` a `<fieldset>`.
        "useSemanticElements": "off",
        // 1 site, and the most interesting of the three: `NoteList.tsx` gives
        // `<button>` — interactive — the non-interactive role `listitem`.
        // Restructuring to `<ul><li><button>` is the real answer.
        "noInteractiveElementToNoninteractiveRole": "off"
      },
      "suspicious": {
        // 2 sites, both `App.tsx`'s scroll mode: `key={i}` and
        // `key={`${mode}:${i}`}` over `grouped`. **Off because the index key
        // is the mechanism here, not an oversight — this is the one rule in
        // this block whose fix would undo a measured improvement.**
        // Reconciling by *position* is what keeps `decode.ts`'s gate alive
        // across a turn: a key derived from the slot remounts `PageView`
        // whenever slots shift, and a freshly-mounted `PageView` has nothing
        // decoded. `App.tsx`'s `renderGroup` says so where it happens, and
        // `docs/notes.md`'s "The flicker, before and after" put a number on
        // it — 3 of 79 animation frames landed mid-load without the gate, 0
        // of 83 with it.
        //
        // The rule's own premise also fails here. Its bug needs state that
        // survives a shift onto the wrong item, and nothing under this key
        // does: the wrapper `<div>` holds none, `PageView` resets from
        // `[src, buffered]`, and `PageAnnotations` re-measures from
        // `[match]` — whose doc comment already reasons about this very key.
        //
        // An earlier version of this comment called it a possible defect and
        // said it wanted investigating. It was investigated; that was wrong,
        // and pointed the next reader at a fix that would reintroduce the
        // flicker. Recorded rather than quietly deleted.
        "noArrayIndexKey": "off"
      }

      // Deliberately left ON, both with inline suppressions carrying their
      // reasons: `correctness/useExhaustiveDependencies`, which is the rule
      // oxlint was enforcing as `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` and whose four
      // suppressions are load-bearing (see `App.tsx`), and
      // `suspicious/noTsIgnore`, whose two sites in `resolver.ts` must stay
      // `@ts-ignore` rather than becoming `@ts-expect-error` — `docs/ci.md`
      // records why, and biome's own suggested fix would break the build in
      // the state where `crates/taiyaku-wasm/pkg` exists.
    }
  },

  // Off deliberately. Biome's import sorting is a separate opinion from
  // formatting, and turning it on would reorder imports across every file in
  // the same commit that introduces the formatter, making both unreviewable.
  "assist": {
    "actions": {
      "source": {
        "organizeImports": "off"
      }
    }
  }
}
